\chapter{The \code{pattern<>} Compile-Time Regex Engine}
\label{ch:pattern-regex}

\section{A small language for small patterns}

The match table of \chapref{ch:pattern-match} dispatches on a key. Most
keys are enumerations or integers, and comparing them is trivial. But a
lexer, a configuration reader and a protocol decoder all need to dispatch
on \emph{strings}, and a string key that matches exactly one spelling is
rarely useful. What is needed is a key that describes a \emph{set} of
spellings: ``a decimal integer'', ``an identifier'', ``a keyword''.

\DSLtk{} provides such a key in the form of \api{dsl::pattern<S>}, a
compile-time regular expression. The pattern is a template parameter, so
it is part of the type of the clause that uses it, and the matching
function is \code{constexpr}, so a match can be performed during constant
evaluation. A table of pattern keys is therefore entirely a compile-time
object, and the dispatch it performs is a comparison of the runtime key
against a set of compile-time shapes.

The facility is deliberately small. \code{dsl::pattern<S>} occupies a
hundred and seventeen lines, from line 6175 to line 6291, and implements a
recogniser with a deliberately restricted syntax. This chapter describes
that syntax exactly, because the difference between what the syntax
\emph{looks} like and what it \emph{does} is the most important thing a
user of this facility needs to know.

\begin{warnbox}[Two regex engines in one header]
\texttt{DSLtk.hpp} contains two independent regular expression engines,
and they are not interchangeable.
\code{dsl::pattern<>} is a tiny compile-time matcher with a very restricted
syntax. \code{dsl::StaticRegex<>}, declared at line 10363, is a full
Thompson NFA with grouping, alternation, negated classes and anchors.
They are not interchangeable, and the restrictions described in this
chapter do \emph{not} apply to \code{StaticRegex}. The two are compared
feature by feature in Table~\ref{tab:regex-compare}.
\end{warnbox}

\section{The data model}

The type is a template on a \api{dsl::FixedString}, the compile-time string
type of \chapref{ch:fixed-string}:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <FixedString S> struct pattern
{
  static constexpr std::string_view value = S.view ();

  static constexpr bool
  matches (std::string_view input)
  {
    return match_here (S.view (), input);
  }

private:
  //  match_class, match_one, match_here
};
\end{lstlisting}

The public surface is deliberately minimal: one static data member,
\code{value}, which exposes the pattern text for diagnostics, and one
static member function, \code{matches}, which tests a string. There is no
search function, no capture group machinery, no iteration interface, and no
error reporting: the answer is a single \code{bool}.

The three private helpers implement the matcher. \code{match_class}
(line 6202) tests a character against the body of a character class.
\code{match_one} (line 6223) tests a character against a single pattern
element, which may be a class, a dot, or a literal. \code{match_here}
(line 6233) is the recursive core.

\section{The algorithm}

\code{match_here} takes two string views: the pattern that remains to be
matched, and the input that remains to be consumed. Its result is a single
boolean.

The base case is the anchor. When the pattern is exhausted, the match
succeeds if and only if the input is also exhausted:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
if (pat.empty ())
  return str.empty ();
\end{lstlisting}

This single line establishes the most important property of the engine:
\emph{the match is anchored at both ends}. There is no search mode, and
there is no way to ask whether a pattern occurs somewhere inside a longer
string. A pattern matches a string only when it consumes the whole string.

The next step handles a leading circumflex, which is skipped:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
if (pat[0] == '^')
  return match_here (pat.substr (1), str);
\end{lstlisting}

Because the match is already anchored at the start, this is redundant. It
is accepted for compatibility with regular expression dialects in which
the anchor is written explicitly.

The next step parses the first pattern element. If it is an opening
bracket, the class body is extracted up to the first closing bracket; if
there is no closing bracket, the match fails immediately. The quantifier
that follows the element --- if any --- is then read, and the remainder of
the pattern is computed. What remains is to decide how many times the
element may be consumed, and that is where the three branches differ.

The \textbf{unquantified} branch consumes exactly one character:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
if (str.empty () || !match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
  return false;
return match_here (rest, str.substr (1));
\end{lstlisting}

The \textbf{star} branch implements genuine backtracking. It first tries
the tail with zero repetitions; if that fails, it consumes one occurrence
at a time and retries the tail after each consumption:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
if (match_here (rest, str))
  return true;
while (!str.empty () && match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
  {
    str.remove_prefix (1);
    if (match_here (rest, str))
      return true;
  }
return false;
\end{lstlisting}

The \textbf{plus} branch is greedy and does \emph{not} backtrack. It
requires at least one occurrence, consumes as many as it can, and then
matches the tail exactly once:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
if (str.empty () || !match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
  return false;
str.remove_prefix (1);
while (!str.empty () && match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
  str.remove_prefix (1);
return match_here (rest, str);
\end{lstlisting}

The difference between the star and the plus branches is not a matter of
style; it changes the language that is recognised, as the worked traces
below show.

\begin{pseudocode}[The matcher: parsing the first element]
\textbf{function} \code{match_here}(pat, str)\\
\quad \textbf{if} pat is empty \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} str is empty\\
\quad \textbf{if} \code{pat[0]} is \code{^} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{match_here} of (pat without its first character, str)\\
\quad cls \code{<-} empty, advance \code{<-} 1\\
\quad \textbf{if} \code{pat[0]} is \code{[} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad end \code{<-} index of the first \code{]} in pat at or after 1\\
\qquad \textbf{if} no such index exists \textbf{then return} false\\
\qquad cls \code{<-} the characters of pat between 1 and end\\
\qquad advance \code{<-} end plus 1\\
\quad quant \code{<-} none\\
\quad \textbf{if} advance is less than the length of pat and\\
\qquad\qquad \code{pat[advance]} is \code{*} or \code{+} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad quant \code{<-} \code{pat[advance]}\\
\qquad\qquad advance \code{<-} advance plus 1\\
\quad rest \code{<-} the characters of pat from advance onwards
\end{pseudocode}

\begin{pseudocode}[The matcher: the three consumption branches]
\quad \textbf{if} quant is \code{*} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} \code{match_here(rest, str)} \textbf{then return} true\\
\qquad \textbf{while} str is not empty and \code{pat[0]} matches \code{str[0]} \textbf{do}\\
\qquad\qquad remove the first character of str\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{if} \code{match_here(rest, str)} \textbf{then return} true\\
\qquad \textbf{return} false\\
\quad \textbf{if} quant is \code{+} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} str is empty or \code{pat[0]} does not match \code{str[0]} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{return} false\\
\qquad remove the first character of str\\
\qquad \textbf{while} str is not empty and \code{pat[0]} matches \code{str[0]} \textbf{do}\\
\qquad\qquad remove the first character of str\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{match_here(rest, str)}\\
\quad \textbf{if} str is empty or \code{pat[0]} does not match \code{str[0]} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} false\\
\quad \textbf{return} \code{match_here(rest, str without its first character)}
\end{pseudocode}

