\section{The syntax, exactly}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.20\textwidth}L{0.72\textwidth}@{}}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Construct} & \textbf{Behaviour} \\
    \midrule
    Literal character &
    Matches that character. Any character other than the special ones
    below is a literal, including \code{#}, \texttt{\%}, \code{&},
    \texttt{\textbackslash} and the comma. \\
    \code{.} &
    Matches any single character, \emph{including} a newline. \\
    \code{[abc]} &
    A character class. Matches one character that is a member of the set. \\
    \code{[a-z]} &
    A range inside a class. The bounds are inclusive, so \code{[a-z]}
    matches \code{a} and \code{z}. A reversed range such as \code{[z-a]}
    matches nothing. \\
    \code{[-a]}, \code{[a-]} &
    A hyphen at either end of the class body is a literal hyphen. A hyphen
    in the middle is a range separator. \\
    \code{*} &
    Zero or more of the preceding element. The preceding element may be a
    literal, a dot or a class. \\
    \code{+} &
    One or more of the preceding element. Greedy; it does not backtrack. \\
    \code{^} &
    Skipped when it appears at the start of the remaining pattern. Inside
    a class it is a literal member of the class. \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{The constructs that \code{dsl::pattern<>} recognises.}
  \label{tab:pattern-syntax}
\end{table}

The following are \emph{not} constructs. Each of them is an ordinary
character with an ordinary meaning, and using one where a quantifier or an
operator is expected produces a pattern that does not do what the author
intended.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{\code{?} is a literal question mark.} It is not a
quantifier. The pattern \code{"a?"} matches the two-character string
\code{"a?"} and does not match \code{"a"}.
\item \textbf{\code{|} is a literal vertical bar.} There is no
alternation. The pattern \code{"a|b"} matches the three-character string
\code{"a|b"}.
\item \textbf{\code{^} inside a class is a literal member.} There is no
negated class. A class whose body begins with a circumflex is a class
whose first member is the circumflex character: the pattern
\code{"[^a]"} matches a string consisting of the single character
\code{"^"} or the single character \code{"a"}, and nothing else. \\
\item \textbf{\code{$} is a literal dollar sign.} The header's documentation
comment lists \code{$} among the constructs that are ``ignored'', on the
ground that the match is always anchored at the end. The implementation
does not special-case \code{$}, so it is matched as a literal character.
The pattern \code{"a$"} matches \code{"a$"} and not \code{"a"}.
\item \textbf{There are no groups.} A parenthesis is a literal
parenthesis. \code{"(a)"} matches the three-character string \code{"(a)"}.
\item \textbf{There are no escape sequences.} A backslash is a literal
backslash. \texttt{"\textbackslash d"} matches the two-character string
\texttt{"\textbackslash d"} and not a digit.
\end{itemize}

\begin{warnbox}[A doc comment that is half right]
The documentation comment above \code{matches} lists \code{^} and
\code{$} as ``ignored''. The \code{^} half is accurate for a circumflex
at the start of the pattern, where the implementation skips it. The
\code{$} half is not: \code{$} is never special-cased and is therefore a
literal. The behaviour of the shipped code is what the pattern engine
section above describes.
\end{warnbox}

\section{Worked traces}

Three traces make the semantics concrete. In each case the pattern is
fixed and the input varies.

\subsection*{\code{pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches("123")}}

The first call is \code{match_here("[0-9]+", "123")}. The pattern is not
empty and its first character is a bracket, so the class body is read: the
first \code{]} is at index 4, the body is \code{"0-9"}, and the element
ends at index 5. The character at index 5 is \code{+}, so the element is
quantified with a plus, and the rest of the pattern is empty.

The plus branch is taken. The input is not empty, and the first character
\code{"1"} is a member of the class, because \code{"0"} is less than or
equal to \code{"1"} and \code{"1"} is less than or equal to \code{"9"}. The
first character is consumed, leaving \code{"23"}. The greedy loop then
consumes \code{"2"} and \code{"3"}, leaving the empty string, and stops
because the input is empty. The tail is matched: \code{match_here("", "")}
returns true because the pattern is empty and the input is empty.

The result is \code{true}.

\subsection*{\code{pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches("123abc")}}

The same pattern is matched against a longer input. Everything proceeds
identically until the greedy loop reaches the character \code{"a"}, which
is not a member of the class \code{"0-9"}, so the loop stops with the input
still holding \code{"abc"}. The tail is then matched:
\code{match_here("", "abc")} finds the pattern empty and the input
non-empty, and returns false.

The result is \code{false}. The engine did not fail to find the number; it
found the number and then found that the input was not exhausted, and it
refused the match because the pattern is anchored at the end.

\subsection*{\code{pattern<"ab*">::matches("abbb")}}

The pattern has no class, so the first element is the literal \code{"a"},
and the character at index 1 is \code{"b"}, which is not a quantifier. The
element is therefore unquantified, the rest of the pattern is \code{"b*"},
and the exactly-one branch applies: the first character \code{"a"} matches,
so the matcher recurses on \code{match_here("b*", "bbb")}.

Now the pattern is \code{"b*"}: the first element is the literal
\code{"b"}, the character at index 1 is \code{"*"}, and the rest is empty.
The star branch applies. It first tries the tail with zero repetitions:
\code{match_here("", "bbb")} returns false, because the input is not
empty. It then enters the loop. The character \code{"b"} matches the
element, so it is consumed, leaving \code{"bb"}, and the tail is retried
and fails. The loop consumes another \code{"b"}, leaving \code{"b"}, and
the tail fails again. The loop consumes the last \code{"b"}, leaving the
empty string, and the tail \code{match_here("", "")} succeeds.

The result is \code{true}. The star branch tried the tail four times, once
for each possible number of repetitions.

\subsection*{Why the asymmetry matters}

The star branch backtracks and the plus branch does not, and the
consequence is a genuine difference in the recognised language. Consider
the pattern \code{"a+ab"} and the input \code{"aab"}. The plus branch
consumes both \code{"a"} characters greedily, leaving \code{"b"}, and then
matches the tail \code{"ab"} against \code{"b"}, which fails. Because the
plus branch never retries with a smaller consumption count, the match is
rejected, even though the string \code{"aab"} plainly starts with a run of
\code{a}s followed by \code{"ab"}.

The pattern \code{"a*ab"} accepts the same input, because the star branch
tries the tail after each consumption count: after consuming one
\code{"a"} the tail \code{"ab"} matches the remaining \code{"ab"}.

The asymmetry is a real limitation, and it is worth remembering whenever a
pattern mixes a greedy repetition with a suffix that could also be
consumed by the repetition.

\section{Comparing the two engines}

\api{dsl::StaticRegex<Source>} is declared at line 10363 and is a
completely different piece of machinery. It compiles its source into a
Thompson NFA --- a program of \emph{split}, \emph{byte}, \emph{any},
\emph{class}, \emph{begin}, \emph{end} and \emph{accept} instructions --- and
simulates the machine over a byte span. Because the simulation tracks the
\emph{set} of active states rather than a single position, it recognises
the same language as a backtracking engine but in linear time, and it
supports the constructs that a backtracking recogniser needs for that to
be true: groups and alternation.

Table~\ref{tab:regex-compare} compares the two.

