\begin{notebox}[Which one should a reader use?]
If the pattern is a \emph{key of a match table}, use
\code{dsl::pattern<>}: it is a compile-time type, so it composes with the
non-type template parameter of \code{dsl::when<>}, and the whole dispatch
can be evaluated during constant evaluation. \code{dsl::StaticRegex<>} is a
run-time facility for byte streams, and it is the subject of
\chapref{ch:static-regex}. They are not substitutes for one another.
\end{notebox}

\section{A worked example}

Listing~\ref{lst:pattern-complete} classifies tokens into four classes. It
uses \code{dsl::pattern<>} for the recognition and the match table of
\chapref{ch:pattern-match} for the dispatch, which is the natural
combination: the pattern decides what the token is, and the table decides
what to do about it.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp, caption={A complete program that
classifies tokens into Number, Keyword, Identifier and Other using
\code{dsl::pattern<>} and the match table.},
label={lst:pattern-complete}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

enum class LexemeClass
{
  Number,
  Keyword,
  Identifier,
  Other
};

//  Every recogniser below is a constant expression.
constexpr bool
is_number (std::string_view t)
{
  return dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches (t);
}

constexpr bool
is_identifier (std::string_view t)
{
  return dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">::matches (t);
}

constexpr bool
is_keyword (std::string_view t)
{
  return dsl::pattern<"if">::matches (t) || dsl::pattern<"for">::matches (t)
         || dsl::pattern<"while">::matches (t);
}

struct LexerDSL : dsl::DSL<LexerDSL, dsl::PatternMatch>
{
  static constexpr auto classify = dsl::match (
      dsl::when<LexemeClass::Number> (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view) { return std::string{ "Number" }; }),
      dsl::when<LexemeClass::Keyword> (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view) { return std::string{ "Keyword" }; }),
      dsl::when<LexemeClass::Identifier> (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view) { return std::string{ "Identifier" }; }),
      dsl::otherwise (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view) { return std::string{ "Other" }; }));
};

int
main ()
{
  //  The documented semantics, checked by the compiler.
  static_assert (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches ("123"));
  static_assert (!dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches ("123abc"));
  static_assert (dsl::pattern<"ab*">::matches ("abbb"));
  static_assert (!dsl::pattern<"a+ab">::matches ("aab"));
  static_assert (dsl::pattern<"a?">::matches ("a?"));
  static_assert (!dsl::pattern<"a?">::matches ("a"));

  for (auto const *text : { "123", "if", "while", "alpha_7", "@bad", "" })
    {
      LexemeClass c = LexemeClass::Other;
      if (is_number (text))
        c = LexemeClass::Number;
      else if (is_keyword (text))
        c = LexemeClass::Keyword;
      else if (is_identifier (text))
        c = LexemeClass::Identifier;
      std::cout << '"' << text << "\" -> " << LexerDSL::classify (c, text) << '\n';
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}

The program prints

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
"123" -> Number
"if" -> Keyword
"while" -> Keyword
"alpha_7" -> Identifier
"@bad" -> Other
"" -> Other
\end{lstlisting}

Note how the pattern syntax shapes the recognisers. The identifier pattern
\code{"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"} is a class followed by a class with a
star; because the star branch backtracks, the pattern accepts an identifier
of any length, including the single-character minimum. The number pattern
\code{"[0-9]+"} uses a plus, which is safe here precisely because no
suffix follows it: there is nothing for the greedy consumption to conflict
with.

The keyword test is a disjunction of three separate patterns, because the
engine has no alternation. This is verbose, and it is the practical cost of
the restricted syntax. A user who needs a large keyword set should either
enumerate the patterns or use \code{dsl::StaticRegex<>}. A structural
\code{StaticRegex} object also supplies the static \code{matches} protocol
recognized by a match clause, so it can serve as a key. Its NFA matching
work runs at runtime even though the automaton is compiled from a fixed pattern.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{No search mode.} \code{matches} asks whether the \emph{whole}
input is matched. There is no \code{search}, no \code{find}, and no
partial-match entry point. A user who wants to find a pattern inside a
longer text must either write a manual scan or use a different engine.

\item \textbf{No quantifier other than \code{*} and \code{+}.} There is
no \code{?}, no \code{{n,m}}, and no lazy variant. A pattern that needs
an optional element must be written as two patterns.

\item \textbf{No alternation and no groups.} Both are ordinary characters.
Any pattern that appears to use them is matching the punctuation.

\item \textbf{No negated classes.} A leading \code{^} in a class is a
member of the class. A user who wants a negated class must enumerate the
complement, which is only practical for small alphabets --- and for those,
\code{dsl::StaticRegex<>} is the better tool.

\item \textbf{No escapes.} A backslash is a backslash, so a pattern
cannot match a newline, a tab, or a literal \code{*}. A pattern that
contains a backslash matches a backslash followed by whatever follows it.

\item \textbf{The class ends at the first closing bracket.} There is no
syntax for a bracket inside a class, so \code{"[]]"} is the class
containing nothing followed by a literal \code{]}. A class that is never
closed makes the whole match fail, rather than raising an error.

\item \textbf{Backtracking can be exponential.} The star branch retries
the tail at every consumption count, and a pattern such as
\code{"(a|a)*a"} has no grouping, so a pathological input can force a
long search. In practice the patterns used as match-table keys are short
and simple, but the engine offers no bound on the work it does.

\item \textbf{The plus branch does not backtrack.} As the traces show,
\code{"a+ab"} does not match \code{"aab"}. This is a genuine difference
from the star branch and from every mainstream regular expression engine,
and it is the single most likely source of surprise.

\item \textbf{The dot matches a newline.} The engine has no notion of a
line-oriented mode, so \code{"."} is a wildcard over the whole character
set.

\item \textbf{Only \code{*} and \code{+} may follow an element.} A
quantifier applied to a quantifier, as in \code{"a**"}, is parsed as a
quantified literal, not as an error. Similarly, a leading \code{*} is a
literal asterisk.

\item \textbf{No captures and no groups.} The engine reports a single
boolean. A user who needs to know \emph{which} part of the input matched
must use a different facility.

\item \textbf{The pattern is fixed at compile time.} The pattern is a
template parameter, so a pattern that must vary at run time is not
expressible. This is the price of the compile-time dispatch, and it is
usually the right price.

\item \textbf{Empty patterns are legal.} \code{dsl::pattern<"">} matches
the empty string and nothing else, because the base case demands that the
input be empty as well.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

\code{dsl::pattern<>} is a small recogniser with a small syntax, and its
value lies in being a \emph{type}: a pattern used as a match-table key
becomes part of the clause's type, and the dispatch it drives can be
evaluated during constant evaluation. Its restrictions --- no alternation,
no groups, no negation, no \code{?}, and a plus that does not backtrack ---
are the price of a hundred lines of code that fit inside a header and work
on every conforming compiler.

The next chapter leaves the vocabulary of values and dispatch behind and
turns to the representation of programs themselves.
\chapref{ch:astnode} introduces \api{dsl::ASTNode}, the value-type tree
that the parsing chapters build and the rewriting chapters transform.
